## The problem

A customer emails on a Tuesday. She pasted an issue link into the wrong Slack channel, someone from a different company clicked it, and it opened. Full issue: title, description, comments, the roadmap it belonged to. Her competitor's employee read her Q3 plan.

The code that caused it looks completely reasonable:

```ts
// GET /issues/:id
const issue = await getIssueById(req.params.id);
if (!issue) return res.status(404).json({ error: "Not found" });
return res.json(issue);
```

The user was authenticated. The token was valid. Nothing threw. The endpoint answered exactly the question it was asked, which was "give me this issue," and nobody ever asked "is this person allowed to see *this* issue."

That is the bug that ends companies, and it's a bug of omission. There is no line to point at in a code review. You have to notice the line that isn't there.

## The idea

Three questions get collapsed into one word, "auth," and separating them is most of the work.

**Authentication** is *who are you*. A valid signed token. Answered once, in middleware, at the edge.

**Authorization** is *may you perform this verb*. Can a viewer delete an issue? This depends on the actor's role, not on which row they picked.

**Tenancy isolation** is *may you touch this row at all*. Does this issue belong to an organization you're a member of? This depends on the row, and it is the one people forget.

Think of an office building. The badge reader at the entrance is authentication. Your badge level opening the server room is authorization. And your company renting only the fourth floor is tenancy. Fail the third and a perfectly valid badge, at a perfectly appropriate level, opens someone else's office.

Roles get simpler when you stop enumerating combinations and give them numbers.

```
owner        50   billing, delete the org, manage admins
admin        40   manage members, all projects
project_lead 30   manage assigned projects, delete issues
member       20   create and edit issues, comment
viewer       10   read only
```

"At least member level" becomes `userLevel >= 20`. Adding a role between two existing ones is a new number, not an audit of every conditional in your codebase.

## In practice

Membership is a table, and it is the only source of truth about who belongs to what.

```sql
CREATE TABLE org_members (
  org_id     uuid NOT NULL REFERENCES organizations(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
  user_id    uuid NOT NULL REFERENCES auth.users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
  role       text NOT NULL CHECK (role IN ('owner','admin','project_lead','member','viewer')),
  created_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
  PRIMARY KEY (org_id, user_id)
);

CREATE INDEX org_members_user_idx ON org_members (user_id);
```

Then write the check exactly once, as a factory: a function that takes configuration and returns a middleware.

```ts
const ROLE_LEVELS = {
  owner: 50, admin: 40, project_lead: 30, member: 20, viewer: 10,
} as const;

export function requireRole(minimumRole: keyof typeof ROLE_LEVELS) {
  return async (req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction) => {
    const orgId = req.params.orgId;
    if (!orgId) return res.status(400).json({ error: "org_id is required" });

    const membership = await findMembership(orgId, req.user.id);
    if (!membership) return res.status(403).json({ error: "Not a member of this organization" });

    if (ROLE_LEVELS[membership.role] < ROLE_LEVELS[minimumRole]) {
      return res.status(403).json({ error: `Requires ${minimumRole}` });
    }

    req.orgId = orgId;
    req.orgRole = membership.role;
    next();
  };
}
```

The outer function runs once at startup and captures the minimum role. The inner one runs per request. That's the whole trick, and it's the same shape as a React hook factory or a Redux middleware.

Now the routes declare their own requirements, and no handler contains an authorization `if`:

```ts
router.get   ("/orgs/:orgId/projects/:pid/issues", requireRole("viewer"),       listIssues);
router.post  ("/orgs/:orgId/projects/:pid/issues", requireRole("member"),       createIssue);
router.delete("/orgs/:orgId/issues/:id",           requireRole("project_lead"), deleteIssue);
```

Read that block top to bottom and you can audit the permissions of an entire resource in five seconds. Compare it to the same policy expressed as copy-pasted checks inside twelve handlers, where the bug is always in the one that got pasted before the policy changed.

**Then do it again in the database.** The middleware is the front door. RLS is the load-bearing wall, and it holds when someone comes in through a window: a background job, a psql session, a new endpoint written on a Friday by someone who didn't know the convention.

A single-owner policy compares one column. An org-scoped policy joins through membership.

```sql
ALTER TABLE issues ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;

CREATE POLICY issues_select_org_member ON issues FOR SELECT
  USING (
    project_id IN (
      SELECT p.id
        FROM projects p
        JOIN org_members om ON om.org_id = p.org_id
       WHERE om.user_id = auth.uid()
    )
  );
```

Now re-read the leaking endpoint from the top of this module. With that policy in place, `getIssueById()` for another org's issue returns zero rows, and your existing `if (!issue) return 404` handles it correctly by accident. The row didn't exist, as far as this user is concerned. That is what defense in depth actually buys: the missing check stops being a breach and becomes a 404.

Two things to notice about that policy. It has no `org_id` parameter, because it never trusts a value the caller supplied. And it's `FOR SELECT` only, which means with RLS enabled and no other policy, `UPDATE` and `DELETE` on this table are denied for everyone. RLS denies by default once enabled. The dangerous state is not "a missing policy," it's **a table where you never enabled RLS at all.**

## In your world

`project-tracker-api` is this module end to end. `src/middleware/rbac.ts` has the factory, the migration has the `check_org_role()` function and the membership-joining policies, and `src/routes/issues.ts` shows the declarations. Read the middleware for one detail worth arguing about: it reads `orgId` from the route param, the query string, *or* the body. The body is attacker-controlled data. It happens to be safe here, because the code then verifies membership in that org rather than trusting the value. Make sure you can explain why that distinction is the thing keeping it safe, because the same pattern without the membership lookup is a vulnerability.

Your work backend is multi-tenant in a different shape: many users rather than many orgs, with the same structural answer. Access flows through a request-scoped database handle that carries the caller's identity, so RLS applies to every query a route makes. There is also a privileged handle that bypasses RLS, reserved for background jobs that legitimately act on all users' data. The reason that distinction is guarded so carefully is exactly this module: using the privileged handle in a request path turns a tenancy check you thought you had into one you don't.

## Watch out

- Roles baked into a JWT go stale. Demote someone from admin and their existing token still claims admin until it expires. Either look up the role per request, or keep tokens short and accept the window deliberately.
- `403` versus `404` leaks information, because "Forbidden" confirms the resource exists. For cross-tenant access `404` is the more honest answer, and RLS gives you it for free.
- Middleware protects routes and nothing else: job handlers, admin scripts, database consoles, and any connection using a service key never run it. The service-role key also bypasses RLS, and table owners bypass their own policies unless you `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY`, so test policies as a real authenticated user.
- Every join in an RLS policy runs on every query against that table. Index `org_members(user_id)` and `projects(org_id)` or your security model becomes your performance problem.

